Keep the EDID color preset on the display's default colorimetry

cm = "edid" renders in the primaries the display's EDID reports. Its image
description leaves primariesNamed at BT.2020 as a placeholder, with
primariesNameSet false, but wantsWideColor() looks only at primariesNamed. On
a display whose EDID also lists BT.2020 in its Colorimetry Data Block, the
output's Colorspace became BT.2020 RGB, and outside 10 bit the "Wide color
gamut is enabled but the display is not in 10bit mode" warning showed on
every hotplug.

Take BT.2020 as wide color only when the preset names it (cm = "wide"). HDR is
unchanged.

--- a/src/output/Monitor.cpp
+++ b/src/output/Monitor.cpp
@@ -2348,7 +2348,9 @@
 }
 
 bool CMonitor::wantsWideColor() {
-    return supportsWideColor() && (wantsHDR() || m_imageDescription->value().primariesNamed == CM_PRIMARIES_BT2020);
+    // The EDID preset names BT.2020 without setting it: it renders in the display's own primaries, which the display takes as its default
+    const auto& desc = m_imageDescription->value();
+    return supportsWideColor() && (wantsHDR() || (desc.primariesNameSet && desc.primariesNamed == CM_PRIMARIES_BT2020));
 }
 
 bool CMonitor::wantsHDR() {
